deep.reconstruction:
Setting and notation. The page works in a tannakian category M of motives over a field k ⊂ ℂ. It uses three fibre functors:
- T_B, Betti, defined over ℚ;
- T_DR, de Rham, defined over k;
- T_Hdg, the Hodge-graded functor gr_F T_DR, defined over k.

G', G'' and G_k are the corresponding motivic Galois groups, all of dimension g. The page introduces three bitorsors under these groups, the 'period torsors':
- P = Isom^⊗(T_B⊗k, T_DR);
- P' = Isom^⊗(T_DR, T_Hdg), which contains the subtorsor Q;
- P'' = Isom^⊗(T_Hdg, T_B⊗k).

These come with composition maps P×P'→P'' and so on. Over ℂ there are canonical points:
- α ∈ P(ℂ), the comparison isomorphism T_B⊗ℂ → T_DR⊗ℂ;
- β ∈ Q(ℂ), the Hodge decomposition, which splits the Hodge filtration over ℂ and so gives T_DR⊗ℂ ≅ gr_F⊗ℂ;
- γ ∈ P''(ℂ), closing the triangle.

Their composite is the identity, which gives βα = γ^{-1} and its cyclic variants. Hence K := k(α,β) = k(β,γ) = k(γ,α). β is essentially obtained from α and complex conjugation, so K is roughly k(α, ᾱ), i.e. periods together with their complex conjugates.

Group theory. The Hodge filtration is a ⊗-filtration of T_DR. Its stabilizer B' ⊂ G' is a parabolic subgroup with:
- unipotent radical U';
- Levi quotient L' = B'/U'.

The grading of gr_F gives a central cocharacter j: G_m → L' (the 'Tate' cocharacter). The weight character ε: G' → G_m satisfies ε∘j = id, so j is a monomorphism. Q is the bundle of Levi subgroups of B', equivalently the lifts j'_0 of j to B'; L' = Centr(j'_0). This makes Q a torsor of relative dimension u under the unipotent radical.

The ℚ-varieties attached to the Betti side are:
- Fil = Filt_⊗(T_B) in the type of the Hodge filtration. This is a flag variety (compact-dual type) of dimension g−b.
- Big = Bigr_⊗(T_B), the gradings or bigradings of Hodge-decomposition type. This is G/L, of dimension g−l.

The forgetful map p: Big → Fil is a torsor under a unipotent group of relative dimension u, so u + l = b.

The first NB makes Fil and Big precise without integrality conditions. One takes larger varieties F ⊃ Fil and Φ ⊃ Big and forms the quotients A = F/G and Φ/G. Fil and Big are then the fibres over the generic points φ_1, ψ_1 of the images of the period points. These images lie in A(k_1), with k_1 the algebraic closure of ℚ in k, and F → Φ induces A ≅ B.

The period points themselves are:
- φ = q(α) ∈ Fil(ℂ), the Hodge filtration transported to Betti, i.e. the period point in the compact dual;
- ψ = r(γ) ∈ Big(ℂ), the Hodge decomposition on T_B⊗ℂ;
- these satisfy φ = p(ψ).

Transcendence-degree bounds. A field-tower diagram records upper bounds from fibre dimensions:
- k(α) over k(φ): at most b;
- k(γ) over k(ψ): at most l;
- k(ψ) over k(φ): at most u;
- k(φ) over k: at most g−b;
- ℚ(φ) over ℚ: at most g−b;
- k(β) over k: at most u;
- K over k(α): at most u;
- K over k(β): at most g.

ℚ(φ_1) is algebraic over ℚ and independent of the embedding of k. The page also notes (2πi)^{±1} ∈ k(α) when the Tate objects ℚ(−n) lie in M.

'Strong transcendence conjecture'. For k ⊂ ℚ̄, trdeg_k K = g + u. It forces all the bounds to be equalities:
- trdeg k(α)/k = g (Grothendieck's period conjecture);
- trdeg k(γ)/k = g;
- trdeg k(β)/k = u;
- trdeg K/k(β) = g;
- trdeg K/k(α) = trdeg K/k(γ) = u;
- trdeg ℚ(φ)/ℚ = g−b, so the period point is 'generic' in the Mumford–Tate compact dual;
- trdeg ℚ(ψ)/ℚ = g−b+u = g−l.

The next page specializes this to elliptic curves and Schneider's theorem. There g = 4, b = 3, u = 1, l = 2, so the conjecture predicts:
- τ is transcendental for non-CM curves (Schneider);
- τ and τ̄ are algebraically independent;
- the periods, quasi-periods and their complex conjugates have transcendence degree 5.

The purpose of the page is to refine Grothendieck's period conjecture so that it also covers the Hodge filtration and Hodge decomposition, i.e. the complex-conjugate (real-structure) data.
